    <!-- Recommendations Descriptions -->
    <string name="rec_desc_0">Contact 106 (SAMU) or 116 (Firefighters) immediately.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_1">Place the patient on their side (Recovery Position) to prevent choking.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_2">Monitor breathing, pulse, and alertness every 5 minutes.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_3">Do not administer anything by mouth (neither water nor pills).</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_4">If the person does not respond and is not breathing, start strong and fast chest compressions.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_5">Apply strong and sustained pressure on the wound with clean cloth to stop bleeding.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_6">If there is an embedded knife or glass, DO NOT remove it. Fix it so it doesn\'t move.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_7">Only if direct pressure fails and limb bleeding is massive, use a high and tight tourniquet.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_8">Clean the edges of the wound with plenty of running water and neutral soap.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_9">Elevate the bleeding arm or leg above heart level.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_10">Pour room temperature water (never ice) over the burn for at least 15 minutes.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_11">Remove rings, bracelets, or tight clothing near the burn before it swells.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_12">Cover the affected area with clear plastic film or clean gauze without pressing.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_13">Prohibited to pop blisters; they are a natural barrier against infections.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_14">Apply ice wrapped in cloth for 15-minute periods to reduce the hematoma.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_15">If a fracture is suspected, immobilize the area using cardboard or boards fixed with bandages.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_16">Clean scrapes by gently dragging away dirt to avoid traumatic tattooing.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_17">Soak the ingrown nail area in warm salt water for 15 minutes to soften the tissue.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_18">Do not try to cut the infected nail with household nail clippers. Requires sterilized material.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_19">Use warm cloths in fold areas (armpits, groin, forehead) to lower fever.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_20">Go urgently if there is neck stiffness, purple spots on the skin, or extreme lethargy.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_21">In case of known allergy and asphyxia, assist the patient in using their injector if they have one.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_22">Take the product container to the hospital for the exact antidote.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_23">In case of ingestion of acids, bleach, or hydrocarbons, it is forbidden to induce vomiting.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_24">Remove the bee stinger by scraping with a card; do not pinch it with tweezers.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_25">Place the pregnant woman lying on her left side to oxygenate the baby.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_26">In seniors and young children, cover with blankets to avoid heat loss.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_27">Do not administer painkillers; they can hide the clinical picture and delay the real diagnosis.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_28">Go to the Emergency Room of the Highest Level Hospital available.</string>
    <string name="rec_desc_29">Schedule an appointment by External Consultation (General Medicine). It is not a vital emergency.</string>

